:answer/id #uuid "ea6a533d-81d4-54a3-ad79-107b7fb6b7e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e322b1a-cf67-58c8-b49a-59002f90e4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593568} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c16a5b3-d46d-5864-9213-9e5e7ad72056" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac941ac-26cc-502d-b8cc-b7058d37dfd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593480} {:answer/id #uuid "7d84dbc6-a816-5a3e-9b6f-c0cab7e46b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593676} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cd8e8e-d77a-53b8-bcef-bd8bf5a09858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d84dbc6-a816-5a3e-9b6f-c0cab7e46b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593676} :answer-field/id #uuid "a13720be-6dfc-56ff-b30c-ffac285beb82" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d427f29-b6d6-5714-998a-bd6087084aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593519} {:answer/id #uuid "d1347ee1-afe3-55fa-b9b3-3615b7b66c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593876} {:answer/id #uuid "da060e0b-338c-5eff-ba8c-4a424ac0306d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593895} {:answer/id #uuid "db28d572-c5d0-5d99-b861-817bf67e4d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d427f29-b6d6-5714-998a-bd6087084aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593519} :answer-field/id #uuid "f30f7129-5221-5fc5-8cb2-b086e87739a0" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd240541-da69-5f1e-b10e-57cf984c2241" :question/math-academy-id "q-182662" :question/problem "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}} &={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ is true for all {{field-9}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}} &=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{1}} &=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}})+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}(1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{k+1}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{{2}^{i}}=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ is true for all $n\\ge 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155ba72f-3d7d-58c5-993f-1188c36455e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593429} {:answer/id #uuid "30e28dec-fa23-5b7f-9951-fee0e5844de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593492} {:answer/id #uuid "3920bbbd-260b-58f8-936a-7e75927e44a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593506} {:answer/id #uuid "7b592af0-5c74-5889-89a9-b3b159179d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593668} {:answer/id #uuid "bb54513e-d2d5-58b8-9412-15eed13734e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b592af0-5c74-5889-89a9-b3b159179d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593668} :answer-field/id #uuid "5efc2d6a-f8ad-5667-a1ec-f91f444b3514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df202318-e036-5725-91a7-7b2e2e6925e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143624" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2x(2-1) \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{e}^{y}}{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) & =P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{e}^{y}]}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3/2)}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d95be1-9570-5a00-a102-d264aff4e1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593437} {:answer/id #uuid "49bac261-ae12-51ff-8c42-c331e9bce4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593557} {:answer/id #uuid "4e357132-43b8-5ae7-994c-39c13a13ac57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593569} {:answer/id #uuid "82ac5fe2-3210-5b78-abfa-235c935b281e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593689} {:answer/id #uuid "d6eaf161-1e68-52ab-b429-22846c9e03fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49bac261-ae12-51ff-8c42-c331e9bce4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593557} :answer-field/id #uuid "e21b54d5-b641-576f-92cf-bde2d4c9f57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8e3e751-672b-5965-af1d-da39efcbadf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-148334" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+2}{4}, & \\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{12},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.5713,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2887,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the correlation coefficient of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{xy+2}{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+2xy)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}-(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}-\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{72}\\right)}{(0.5713)\\cdot (0.2887)} \\\\  & ≈0.08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2adf7dc2-b121-5b2d-a09e-876fee16c458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(1-{x}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593481} {:answer/id #uuid "4c30ed16-d3fe-54a4-96d0-29778839ed94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593561} {:answer/id #uuid "5676c57c-88a7-5e17-bad9-412cdebaa995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy(1-{x}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593585} {:answer/id #uuid "7645cc41-db55-5ec2-b263-2eb627b7bd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{2(1-{x}^{4})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593653} {:answer/id #uuid "9519fd2b-3db5-5c5e-b8fc-de56c98df6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{1-{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c30ed16-d3fe-54a4-96d0-29778839ed94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593561} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4583ae-8ad1-545d-860e-496b4f5437ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4a2e6a7-5117-5150-b7b8-3b6ab1df9427" :question/math-academy-id "q-143639" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{{x}^{2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot (1-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{21{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{4})}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{4}}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fcfa4b7-c916-54c9-a953-58ca4ac1f19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593450} {:answer/id #uuid "656295f1-ebd3-58c2-bb8c-765344439ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1}{3({y}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593612} {:answer/id #uuid "68afc157-3036-5831-9789-faf8c13fd136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593620} {:answer/id #uuid "dca54d9d-a1e2-512e-8148-62f293d15c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(xy+1)}{y+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593902} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4be62b-7ba9-5e60-84c4-1b7fda7634c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}(xy+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68afc157-3036-5831-9789-faf8c13fd136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186593620} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f8ff5d-95a8-5094-a7ea-98c2e2bfaffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcf706c6-7e10-5c3f-b0cf-d9c199f24a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-144945" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{16}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({y}^{2}+3)}{16}, & 1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{16}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3({y}^{2}+3)}{16}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{{y}^{2}+3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d4efc6-1069-5ea5-842f-8aaa08f4905e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594350} {:answer/id #uuid "8b675762-96e4-5e45-acf0-873b4018a5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594466} {:answer/id #uuid "b317f905-88f2-5087-bb2a-9f1719e2d250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594508} {:answer/id #uuid "ea55f8fb-e8fd-5801-a571-a15b6b722358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594564} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e3c304-5647-510a-9b1f-410ce164475b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14d4efc6-1069-5ea5-842f-8aaa08f4905e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594350} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbecdde-2e50-536d-babb-3eba966fe9ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dddc257-8e4b-5d9e-b703-54f233755f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-270035" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+2x+3 & =-({x}^{2}-2x)+3 \\\\  & =-(({x}^{2}-2x+1)-1)+3 \\\\  & =-((x-1)^{2}-1)+3 \\\\  & =-(x-1)^{2}+1+3 \\\\  & =4-(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{4-(x-1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{4(1-(x-1)^{2}/4)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}/{2}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x-1)/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\frac{x-1}{2}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-1}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $1-\\sqrt{3}$ | $1+\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{1+\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x-1)/2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}/2}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}/2}^{\\sqrt{3}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-\\sqrt{3}/2}^{\\sqrt{3}/2} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51acaaac-baef-5803-8cd1-b86423feaec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594407} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fd336b-b949-56cb-bd60-34b48d3edf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6x-2}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594488} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f07415-388a-512f-b356-5de71daac17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6x-2}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594512} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f3c0ab-7a7c-5a85-bff6-db5bb11da37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6x-2}{6}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594513} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5dd17a-6795-5dfe-a910-b6de106bc691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8f07415-388a-512f-b356-5de71daac17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6x-2}{3}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594512} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e73e59-e007-5c42-be39-4876643435c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fcd9fc3-371b-5673-8156-b57ae924d2dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80308" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{(6x-2)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-(6x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9(1-(6x-2)^{2}/9)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(6x-2)^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{1-(6x-2)^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x-2)^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x-2)/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\frac{6x-2}{3}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{6x-2}{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-(6x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x-2)/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6x-2}{3}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186594674 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df5fa9c-a5e1-508d-a181-de8c02678f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594395} {:answer/id #uuid "544e3d16-e1ea-53ca-abbc-27231cee8114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594411} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff8ee0c-7102-53f1-8ec6-6a03f2d71611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594437} {:answer/id #uuid "90063b1a-c6dc-543d-9114-77f15982ded7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594471} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a7a5f6-97d0-5b52-a441-39da29be536b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3df5fa9c-a5e1-508d-a181-de8c02678f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594395} :answer-field/id #uuid "d386df52-1c9a-54d0-984e-a271d6b24d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186594650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c84f753-589b-5ce1-9459-1771a16344eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-36697" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x<\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡0 \\\\  & =2(1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, we have $x>\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
